General elections were held in the [[Dominican Republic]] on 5 July 2020 to elect a president, vice-president, 32 senators and 190 deputies. They had originally been planned for 17 May, but were postponed due to the [[2020 coronavirus pandemic in the Dominican Republic|coronavirus pandemic]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Padrón para las elecciones de mayo tendrá un incremento de más de 69 mil electores|url=https://listindiario.com/la-republica/2020/01/14/599965/padron-para-las-elecciones-de-mayo-tendra-un-incremento-de-mas-de-69-mil-electores|date=14 January 2020|access-date=14 January 2020|publisher=Listin Diario|language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=La JCE pospone las elecciones presidenciales y congresuales para el 5 de julio|url=https://www.diariolibre.com/actualidad/politica/la-jce-pospone-las-elecciones-presidenciales-y-congresuales-para-el-5-de-julio-OI18231681|date=14 April 2020|access-date=14 April 2020|publisher=Diario Libre|language=es-ES}}</ref> They are the second elections since [[1994 Dominican Republic general election|1994]] in which all positions will be elected simultaneously, and the first in Dominican history in which all authorities will be elected simultaneously and directly.<ref>{{cite news|title=Elecciones de 2020 son un reto para la JCE|url=http://eldia.com.do/elecciones-de-2020-son-un-reto-para-la-jce/|date=2 August 2017|access-date=2 December 2017|publisher=El Día|language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Este será el orden de los partidos en la próxima boleta electoral|url=https://listindiario.com/la-republica/2019/12/10/595237/este-sera-el-orden-de-los-partidos-en-la-proxima-boleta-electoral|date=10 December 2019|access-date=10 December 2019|publisher=Listin Diario|language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Candidatos presidenciales podrán gastar más de 900 millones durante campaña electoral|url=https://listindiario.com/la-republica/2020/03/17/608995/candidatos-presidenciales-podran-gastar-mas-de-900-millones-durante-campana-electoral|date=17 March 2020|access-date=17 March 2020|publisher=Listin Diario|language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=La JCE aprueba tres formatos distintos para boletas de mayo|url=https://listindiario.com/la-republica/2020/03/19/609271/la-jce-aprueba-tres-formatos-distintos-para-boletas-de-mayo|date=19 March 2020|access-date=19 March 2020|publisher=Listin Diario|language=es-ES}}</ref>

Incumbent President [[Danilo Medina]] was ineligible to stand for re-election, having served two consecutive terms since 2012. The governing [[Dominican Liberation Party]]'s 16-year rule ended after [[Modern Revolutionary Party]] candidate [[Luis Abinader]] received a majority of the vote.<ref name=newpresident>{{cite news|url=https://news.yahoo.com/dominican-republic-votes-election-postponed-004941501.html|title=Change in Dominican Republic as opposition wins presidency|author=BBC News|publisher=Yahoo News|date=6 July 2020|access-date=6 July 2020}}</ref> Rival candidates [[Gonzalo Castillo (politician)|Gonzalo Castillo]] and [[Leonel Fernández]] also conceded defeat.<ref name=newpresident /> The Modern Revolutionary Party also won a majority of seats in the Senate and a plurality in the Chamber of Deputies. The election was a partial realignment, with the Modern Revolutionary Party entering a status as a major party in the country, replacing the [[Dominican Revolutionary Party]], who saw poor election results for the second election in a row and who obtained its lowest total vote share and seat count in its history. Abinader would be officially sworn in as President on 16 August.<ref name=presidentnow />

==Electoral system==
The President of the Dominican Republic is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives 50% + 1 vote, or more, of the total votes, a second-round [[Two-round system|runoff]] will be held between the two candidates with the highest votes on the first round.

The 32 members of the [[Senate of the Dominican Republic|Senate]] are elected from the 31 provinces and the [[Distrito Nacional]] using [[first-past-the-post voting]].<ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2094_B.htm Senado (Senate)] IPU</ref>

The 190 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies of the Dominican Republic|Chamber of Deputies]] are elected in three groups; 178 are elected by proportional representation from 32 multi-member constituencies based on the 31 [[provinces of the Dominican Republic|provinces]] and the [[Distrito Nacional]], with the number of seats based on the population of each province. A further seven members are elected by proportional representation by Dominican expatriates in 3 [[overseas constituencies]], and five seats are allocated at the national level to parties that received at least 1% of the vote nationally, giving preference to those that did not win any of the 178 constituency seats.<ref name=IPU>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2093_B.htm Cámara de Diputados (Chamber of Deputies)] IPU</ref>

The 20 seats in the [[Central American Parliament]] are elected by proportional representation.

